A||||9||Uaana||Help! No Start||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||10-24-2001||10:43 PM||||Ok got a wierd one for all you tech types.<BR>When the temp dips below 40f all the car will do is click the solenoid. but will not engage the actual starter motor. <BR>The battery is brand new. connections were checked in the shop just yesterday. <BR>And the damn thing works fine once the temp rises above 40 degrees..<P>Suggestions? Hints?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Turbo88||10-24-2001||11:15 PM||||You say the battery is new.. is it possible it is bad out of the box? How about the starter? Maybe years of road grime built up on the bushings are preventing the motor from getting enough power (long shot)? <P>Just to check the battery, try bringing it in overnight so it stays warm and throwing it in the next morning. If it turns right over, I would guess you might have a battery that is bad out of the box. Only takes a second to yank it out.. That would be my first suggestion. Beyond that, have your starter checked. Just because a shop says your connections are solid doesn't make it so unless you really trust them. Fiero's battery connections are fairly easy to get to.<P>Good Luck...<P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||falconhulk||10-25-2001||07:59 AM||||Yes sounds like the battery becasue the starter actually runs BETTER in the cold.  Batterys have whats called internal resistance.  When the temp goes down this resistance goes up.  What this does it 1 limit the amount of current available to the starter and 2 produces less voltage to the starter due to the voltage drop across the internal resistance.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Uaana||10-25-2001||09:47 AM||||If it was the battery, then why won't it start with a jump?||||reg||1
Z||000004||b52buf||10-25-2001||10:48 AM||||You might want to check your battery cables.<BR>Sometimes they corrode inside the insulation.<BR>This builds up a resistance and with the engine cold might contribute to the starting problem.If the cables are orininal it might help to change them. I've seen alot of good starters changed only to find out it's the much cheaper cables.Check both + and - cables.||||reg||1
Z||000005||falconhulk||10-25-2001||10:53 AM||||Yes its also posible its the cables ......under normal conditions the battery can out out enough juice to over come the cble resistance but with the cold it cannot.||||reg||1
Z||000006||b52buf||10-25-2001||11:04 AM||||After reading your second post, "won't start with a jump". I'd have the battery checked at a shop. Sears will do this for a fee $  (used to be free).They can check the charging and cranking systems. Right now I'm thinking starter but you don't want to do that unless it's needed.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Adrift||10-25-2001||11:25 AM||||I've been having the same problem - Canadian tire in Canada will check your battery for free - they check my and said it was okay so I guess it could be the starter or its wiring... going to check that today<BR>Ari<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||10-25-2001||12:25 PM||||Check out the resistance from the bat to the stud where the cable mounts to the starter/solenoid to see if your cable is okay internally.  Also, you may need to take the solenoid off the starter and clean it up inside.  There is a slight chance it is the batt but I doubt it.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000009||falconhulk||10-25-2001||12:40 PM||||There would be no reason for the solenoid to not work below 40 degrees!  That would be the first I ever heard of that.  The onlt thing the cold could effect would be the battery.  But even then 40 degrees is not that cold.||||reg||1
